  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13972835/assets/ecf128d341a8a568ade4abaa2a59e1083d2c436fdb3d2a0b4b642fb7e85cee63.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?",
  "worked_solution": "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the left. The parabola has its vertex at $(6,4)$ and passes through the point $(1,9).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h,k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(6,4),$ we substitute $h=6$ and $k=4$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-4)^{2} & =4p(x-6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(1,9),$ so we can substitute $x=1$ and $y=9$ into the equation above and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-4)^{2} & =4p(x-6) \\\\ (9-4)^{2} & =4p(1-6) \\\\ 25 & =-20p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-4)^{2} & =4p(x-6) \\\\ (y-4)^{2} & =4(-\\frac{5}{4})(x-6) \\\\ (y-4)^{2} & =-5(x-6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the left since $p=-\\frac{5}{4}$ is negative.",
